\chapter{Embedding MuLL in C++}
Include the runtime header and, when needed, the standard-library installer.
Construct one runtime per session, register native services, then run source.
\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include "mull/stdlib/Stdlib.hpp"

int main() {
    mull::Runtime vm({"/opt/myapp/mull/stdlib"});
    mull::stdlib::install(vm);
    vm.stdoutSink = [](std::string_view s) { std::cout << s; };
    try {
        auto result = vm.run("return 6 * 7;", "startup.mull");
        return result.number() == 42 ? 0 : 1;
    } catch (const mull::Error &error) {
        std::cerr << error.what() << '\n';
        return 1;
    }
}
\end{code}

Build with a C++20 compiler and the toolkit directory on the include path.
The runtime is header-only. The supplied native libraries are also inline
headers, so no interpreter library needs to be linked separately.

\section{Host bindings}
\api{define(name, value, mutableBinding)} adds a global binding; mutability
defaults to false. \api{get(name)} retrieves a binding's value. Construct
\api{mull::Value} from a Boolean, integer, double, string, list, record, or
\api{Value::Native} callable. A native callable accepts a list of arguments
and returns a value.

\api{runFile(path)} reads and runs a source file with its path in diagnostics.
Runtime objects are noncopyable and nonmovable. Keep the runtime alive while
using script functions returned from it. Calls into a given instance must be
serialized, and recursive entry through \api{run} is rejected; native code
can invoke a supplied function value through its \api{call} method instead.
